---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocFigure from '../../../components/DocFigure.astro';
import { c4Levels, containers, subCapabilityCount, capabilities, containerCatalogue } from '../../../data/architecture';
import { docsNav } from '../../../data/docs';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../../data/content';

const architecture = docsNav.find((s) => s.label === 'Architecture')!;
const onward = architecture.pages.slice(1);

const toc = [
  { id: 'two-models', label: 'Two models, one source' },
  { id: 'four-levels', label: 'The four levels of C4' },
  { id: 'in-one-glance', label: 'The platform in one glance' },
  { id: 'read-next', label: 'What to read next' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Architecture overview — Qubership APIHUB"
  description="How the Qubership APIHUB architecture documentation is organized: the C4 model answers how the platform is built, the capability map answers what it does."
  eyebrow="Architecture"
  heading="Overview"
  lead="APIHUB is described by two models that meet in the middle. The C4 model answers how the platform is built. The capability map answers what it does. Every sub-capability names the container that implements it, and every container appears in the C4 diagrams."
  toc={toc}
>
  <!-- ══ TWO MODELS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="two-models" class="doc-section mb-16">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Two models, one source</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl">
      Architecture documentation usually fails in one of two ways: it describes boxes nobody can map to a
      repository, or it lists features nobody can map to a runtime. APIHUB is documented from both ends, and
      the two descriptions are kept consistent by sharing one model file.
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 mb-8">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-blue-500/25 bg-blue-500/[0.06] p-7">
        <p class="text-xs font-bold text-blue-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">Model one · C4</p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">“How is it built?”</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-5">
          Four levels of zoom, from a single box on a context diagram down to the registries and pipelines
          that matter in the code. Plus a deployment view and three dynamic views.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md text-xs font-medium bg-blue-500/10 text-blue-300 border border-blue-500/20">4 levels</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md text-xs font-medium bg-blue-500/10 text-blue-300 border border-blue-500/20">{containerCatalogue.length} containers</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md text-xs font-medium bg-blue-500/10 text-blue-300 border border-blue-500/20">3 flows</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-violet-500/25 bg-violet-500/[0.06] p-7">
        <p class="text-xs font-bold text-violet-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">Model two · capability map</p>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">“What does it do?”</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-5">
          A capability is a stable statement of what the platform does, so it survives refactoring. Nine of
          them, broken down into {subCapabilityCount} sub-capabilities with technology and maturity overlays.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md text-xs font-medium bg-violet-500/10 text-violet-300 border border-violet-500/20">{capabilities.length} capabilities</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md text-xs font-medium bg-violet-500/10 text-violet-300 border border-violet-500/20">{subCapabilityCount} sub-capabilities</span>
          <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md text-xs font-medium bg-violet-500/10 text-violet-300 border border-violet-500/20">2 overlays</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/40 p-6">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        <span class="font-semibold text-white">Where they meet.</span> The technology overlay on the
        capability map colours each sub-capability by the container that owns it. Change the model once and
        both descriptions follow — which is why the capability data on this site is imported from the same
        file that generates the architecture slide deck.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ FOUR LEVELS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="four-levels" class="doc-section mb-16">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The four levels of C4</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-2 max-w-3xl">
      Each level answers exactly one question and deliberately hides the detail of the next. If a diagram
      makes you ask “but what is inside that box?”, the answer is on the level below.
    </p>

    <DocFigure
      src="images/architecture/c4-levels.svg"
      alt="The four levels of the C4 model: context, containers, components and code, each one nested inside the previous"
      caption="C4 · four levels of zoom, each hiding the detail of the next"
    />

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4">
      {c4Levels.map((level) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/30 p-5">
          <span
            class="inline-block px-2 py-0.5 rounded text-[11px] font-extrabold mb-3"
            style={`background:${level.color}; color:#050813;`}
          >
            {level.id}
          </span>
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">{level.name}</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">{level.question}</p>
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold" style={`color:${level.color}`}>{level.answer}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ IN ONE GLANCE ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="in-one-glance" class="doc-section mb-16">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The platform in one glance</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6 max-w-3xl">
      Seven containers get deployed. A further twelve TypeScript libraries are not deployed on their own,
      but they are where parsing, diffing and rendering actually happen.
    </p>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      {containerCatalogue.map(({ key, port }) => {
        const c = containers[key];
        return (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/30 p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 mb-2">
              <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-sm flex-shrink-0" style={`background:${c.color}`}></span>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white truncate">{c.name}</h3>
              <span class="ml-auto font-mono text-[10px] text-slate-600 flex-shrink-0">{port}</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mb-3">{c.tech}</p>
            <a
              href={`https://github.com/Netcracker/${c.repo}`}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="font-mono text-[10px] text-slate-600 hover:text-slate-400 transition-colors break-all"
            >
              {c.repo}
            </a>
          </div>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ READ NEXT ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="read-next" class="doc-section mb-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">What to read next</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      {onward.map((page, i) => (
        <a
          href={page.href}
          class="group flex items-start gap-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/30 p-6 hover:border-blue-500/40 transition-colors"
        >
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-slate-700 pt-1 tabular-nums flex-shrink-0">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-1.5 group-hover:text-blue-400 transition-colors">{page.label}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">{page.summary}</p>
          </div>
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-4 h-4 text-slate-700 group-hover:text-blue-400 transition-colors flex-shrink-0 mt-1.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M13.5 4.5 21 12m0 0-7.5 7.5M21 12H3" /></svg>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      These pages are derived from the APIHUB architecture deck (September 2026). The capability data is
      imported from the deck's model file, so the two stay in step. Source code for every container lives
      under <a href={GITHUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-slate-300 transition-colors">Netcracker on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
